WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.780
My name's Gary Hart and I'm running for President.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:07.080
How much is the public entitled to know about
a politician’s personal life?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.640
And, ultimately, who gets to decide?

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.280
These are the questions at the heart of the
new film, The Front Runner.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.750
The film is based on the true story

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:20.180
of Senator Gary Hart’s run for the 1988 democratic
presidential nomination,

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.380
a campaign that derailed after The Miami Herald
reported allegations

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.420
that Hart was having an affair with an model
Donna Rice.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.800
Directed by Jason Reitman,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.680
who co-wrote the screenplay with reporter
Matt Bai

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.280
and former political operative Jay Carson,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.720
the film tells the story of Harts doomed campaign
through the eyes of his family, campaign aides,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.340
and a press corps wrestling with the idea
of what is newsworthy.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.040
Senator, I wanna ask you some questions about
the woman in your townhouse.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.110
Can you tell us how you know her?

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.890
You can't be serious, no one is staying in
my home, there's no need for that.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.149
I am serious sir.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:56.780
I couldn't quite believe there was this moment
in our recent history

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.780
when the presumed next President of the United
States

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.979
wound up in an alleyway, middle of the night,
behind his home with some other journalists

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.180
no one knew what to do, because no one had
ever been there before.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.940
I mean it just sounded like a movie, a thriller.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.540
Why are we standing in an alley on a Saturday
night?

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.700
I mean, don't you think you owe it to use
to be forthcoming.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:16.620
Owe you?

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.160
You're denying what we've seen with our own
eyes!

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.700
The only thing that I deny is the idea that
somehow you have the right to ask me these things.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.780
You're running for President!

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.300
I'm aware of that, Tom, it's in the papers.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.540
Well you have a responsibility!

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.079
I know full well what my responsibilities
are, do you know yours?

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.340
It had all this connective tissue with today,

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.900
all of these questions about our public lives
versus our private lives,

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:38.409
gender politics, our relationship with journalism

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:41.329
and what we want to know about our potential
leaders,

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:44.680
what flaws we're willing to put up with in
our leaders.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.480
And there's something about this moment in
1987

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.759
where we suddenly showed a very deep interest
in Gary Hart

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.500
not only as a politician, not in the way of
someone with ideas

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.580
but someone as a flawed human being who had
made a mistake.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.100
We wanted to know more about that mistake.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.239
We've come to where we live

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.380
in a culture that kind of revolves around
shame in an interesting way.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:13.340
And if you are someone who experiences shame,
you drop out of the race.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.620
But if you are someone who doesn't experience
shame,

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.620
not only do you stay in, but you thrive, so-

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.660
You're drawn into the process!

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.081
You're drawn into the process yeah.

00:02:22.081 --> 00:02:26.600
So it is a process that really supports the
shameless.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.500
But in this story, we don't have good guys
and bad guys

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.750
we're not placing blame on any of the people
in there

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.700
we're trying to show human beings that are
placed into difficult choices

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.080
and are doing the best they can in those circumstances
that no one had ever faced before.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.700
And so we literally tried to show the journalists
as humans, the candidate as human,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.570
the operatives as human.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.850
I got a hundred kids unpacking boxes in Denver
right now

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.170
because we told them you would do what it
takes to win this thing,

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.340
I told them, people are sacrificing for you!

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:03.660
And I'm not sacrificing, what, should I sacrifice
my privacy, Bill, my self respect?

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.200
And it won't just be me that get's dragged
down,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.640
none of those kids you're talking about
will work another campaign

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:11.100
where some candidate doesn't have to account
for who comes and goes to their bedroom

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.630
and that is just the beginning.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.330
Okay, okay but we're not, we're not talking
about that right now-

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:16.550
I am talking about that!

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.500
We're talking about how you get through today

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.020
without pissing away everything we've all
worked for on this campaign.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.740
This campaign is about the future, not rumors,
not sleaze

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.820
and I care about the sanctity of this process
whether you do or do not!

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.120
Between the two of us we spent over 40 years
in politics

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.300
and a broken system broke us.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:43.200
And so, you know, we came to this, this process
has been somewhat cathartic

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.140
and I feel a good bit of catharsis every time
I watch the movie,

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.100
I've seen it over a dozen times now.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:55.060
Because, you know, we're exploring, we're
exploring some of the story

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.480
of how it got so messed up.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.660
It's funny, 'cause I have a similar experience
when I watch it I think

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.660
'could that be me, could I be in the alley,
could I be where Tom Fiedler is?

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.640
could I do, what would I have said in that
Post meeting'

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.640
And I never find a really satisfactory answer
to that.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:12.080
The answer is never 'of course not, I would
never do that.'

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.310
And that's not realistic and so you're always,
to me,

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.220
It's, you can watch it differently and come
away with a different perspective every time.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.400
Yeah, I see the operatives doing stuff that
I used to do and think

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.540
'hell that's no big deal.'

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.830
And I see it on the screen and I go 'ooh that
looks really, that looks really unattractive.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.069
I can't believe I used to do that every day.'

00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:34.780
Reitman's earlier work also resisted passing
judgement on his characters.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:38.120
In an interview about his critically acclaimed
2007 film Juno,

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.120
which tackles teenage pregnancy,

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.700
Reitman said "I'm not really pro choice, I'm
not really pro life

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.990
I'm libertarian and I think people should
make decisions for themselves."

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.480
In his 2005 film, Thank You for Smoking,

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.760
which delves into the alliance between politicians
and big business,

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.640
Reitman doesn't condemn cigarette use, as
he told the Arizona Republic at the time

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.320
"... as a libertarian I don't like government
control.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.520
People should be left to their own devices."

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.240
I mean you've self described in the past as
sort of a libertarian with a heart.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.160
With that sort of world view,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.419
I wonder what kind of discussions you guys
had about this

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.180
when you're hashing out the script and story.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:19.980
I mean, I'm like anyone, I look around and
wonder how the hell we got here.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.680
The conversation is so shrill, you go on twitter
and you just see people tearing each other apart

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:30.660
so I'm, I'm desperately in search of a reasonable
conversation.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:34.719
Yeah I think our discussion as we are all
writing in this sort of iterative process

00:05:34.719 --> 00:05:39.870
it was never about, it was never an argument
over the animating questions or how we felt

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.020
it was more, it was more 'is that real? is
that authentic?

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:48.140
is that the way it would really be? you know,
and is it complex?

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.940
The complicated part about making a movie
in 2018

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.820
is making sure that everyone has an access
point to the story.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.879
Because, you know, it's not as much a movie
about Gary Hart

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:04.620
as much as it is a movie about a group of
people trying to understand Gary Hart, you know.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:10.400
And the first half of the film specifically,
cinematically follows over a dozen people

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.360
at the Washington Post, at the Herald, at
the campaign staff, in his family.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:18.940
Men, women, young, old, veterans, newcomers,

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:24.400
each trying to understand an enigma of a human
being.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.920
Who is not self aware of himself and we kind
of knew that

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.040
alright, each one of these characters that
we're following is gonna give us an access point in

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.800
and sometimes we have to stretch that a little,
you know,

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.640
like Ann Devroy, you know an editor at the
Washington Post,

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:44.180
we gave her more language from 2018 when it
comes to the women's movement

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.900
because that is part of this conversation

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.999
and at that point, while it was important
for us to be accurate to 1987

00:06:50.999 --> 00:06:55.089
it is more important for people now to be
able to watch this film

00:06:55.089 --> 00:06:58.869
and feel as though they hear that voice within
the argument

00:06:58.869 --> 00:07:03.500
and they kind of watch it ping pong off the
other voices within the film.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:10.020
This isn't a movie I think people should or
will walk out of thinking 'now I've got the answer.'

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.060
But it is hopefully a movie where people walk
out saying and we've seen this

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.000
with some of the, you know, initial screenings

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.000
is people walk out saying 'I think he was
wrong. I think he was right.'

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.400
You know, I mean you know those are great
experiences,

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.340
we've all had the experience of leaving with
your, you know,

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:30.580
you're wife or your friend and you're in the
car arguing about what the movie was really about,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:33.280
who was at fault, what the decisions made
that were critical.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.700
I think that's what we're going for here is
to let people think for themselves.

